course: dictionary
lesson: 4207
title: Gossip and kindness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Gossip is talk about other people's private lives, and it is often unkind or not true. Words like this can be tricky, so use the context. «Judge people by how they treat you, not by gossip.» Here gossip is something you should not use to decide what someone is really like. The sentence tells you it is a poor way to judge.
explain: Gossip can hurt feelings and spread rumours that are not true. «Bad-news gossip can feel caring but still spreads fear; offer help, not headlines.» So even when gossip sounds kind, it can still scare people. Ask yourself: is this talk about someone's private life, and could it be untrue?
explain: Gossip is not the same as telling a grown-up about something serious. «And when you are truly worried about a child's safety, that is not gossip: tell a trusted adult, who knows who to talk to.» If someone may be in danger, telling a trusted adult is the right thing to do, and it is not tattling.
fact: Gossip is talk about other people's private lives, often unkind or not true.
fact: A gossip is a person who likes to talk about others. To gossip means to talk about others in this way.
fact: «Spread gossip» means passing it on to other people.
fact: Gossip that hurts can upset feelings and spread untrue rumours, so it is good to stop it rather than pass it on.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about a child who may be in danger is not gossip.
exercise: «Judge people by how they treat you, not by gossip.» What is gossip in this sentence? | A kind word you say to a friend | A type of game played at lunchtime | Good news about a school trip | *Talk about other people's private lives, often unkind or not true
exercise: Ruby is worried that a friend may be in danger. What is the right thing to do? | Wait a week and see what happens | Keep it quiet and tell your friends at lunch | Post it online so everyone knows | *Tell a trusted adult, because that is not gossip
exercise: «Bad-news gossip can feel caring but still spreads fear; offer help, not headlines.» What does this sentence warn you about? | Bad news is always true, so spread it | *Gossip can feel kind but still makes people scared | Gossip always makes people calm and happy | Caring people never talk about news
test: «Accounts that post gossip about real pupils should not be followed or shared; they should be reported.» What should you do with these accounts? | Copy their posts into your own book | Ignore the rules because they are only online | *Report them | Follow and share them so more people see
test: Leo says, «Mia is such a gossip.» What does gossip mean when it describes a person? | A person who keeps secrets very well | *Someone who likes to talk about others | A player in a team game | A kind teacher who helps at school
exam: Mia hears a story about Sam that might not be true. What is the best choice? | *Do not spread it, and judge Sam by how Sam treats you | Believe the story and decide Sam is unkind | Ask a friend who heard it from someone else | Tell everyone at lunch because it is juicy news
